	affiliations = {School of Technology, Software Engineering Research Lab, Dhirubhai Ambani University, Gujarat, Gandhinagar, India},
	abstract = {Use cases are widely accepted and defined as tools for eliciting, specifying and validating software requirements by offering a structured template to capture system interactions and user goals. However, generating high-quality use case descriptions is often challenging, labour-intensive and error-prone when derived from natural language textual specifications. Recent research has explored the potential of large language models (LLMs) in generating use case models in specific domains; however, their applicability across broader software domains remains underexplored. In this paper, we present UCGen, an LLM-based automated approach for generating use case textual descriptions for requirements specifications, considering a human-in-the-loop (HITL) approach. The proposed approach uses different prompting techniques to generate use case descriptions and develops a HITL pipeline to verify the generated use cases. We demonstrated the effectiveness of the UCGen by applying it to ten Public Requirements (PURE) datasets. Our evaluation results showed that the use cases generated using UCGen are, on average, more complete, correct, and less redundant than the human-derived use cases. Additionally, we found that using HITL further improves the quality of use case descriptions. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
