	affiliations = {Department of Computer Engineering, Mukesh Patel School of Technology Management & Engineering, SVKM’s Narsee Monjee Institute of Management Studies (NMIMS) Deemed-to-University, Mumbai, 400056, India},
	abstract = {Use case modelling is an important technique used in software engineering and systems analysis to define, clarify, and organize system requirements from a user’s perspective. A Unified Modelling Language’s (UML) use case diagram is a graphical representation of the interactions between actors (users or external systems) and a system under consideration. One of the primary challenges in use case modelling is the extraction of essential elements for generating use case diagrams from natural language documents. This paper presents an automated approach by leveraging Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques and rule based approach, to syntactically parse specifications and expedite the process. Specifically, the Subject-Verb-Object (SVO) relationship extractor is applied to establish relationships between subjects (actors) and verb + object (use case) pairs within a sentence boundary. The approach identifies relevant actors and use cases, the crucial components of a use case diagram, from the parsed data. The approach is implemented using the SmartNet dataset and validated against state-of-the-art case studies namely Philips and E-store, to evaluate its effectiveness and accuracy in extracting actors and use cases. The performance of the proposed approach on the SmartNet dataset yields an accuracy of up to 84%. In contrast, for the benchmark datasets, the accuracy ranges from 94.85% to 95.23%, demonstrating the effectiveness and robustness of the proposed approach to extract use case elements across different datasets. SVO relationship extraction algorithm and rule-based methods significantly accelerated the process of generating use case diagrams from natural language documents. The ability to handle a wide variety of sentence structures and domain-specific content ensures that the proposed approach is adaptable to different software requirements. This proposed approach addresses a key challenge in use case diagram generation and enhances the process by automating the extraction of actors and use cases from text. The approach performs well on an industry SmartNet dataset along with its successful generalization to other benchmark datasets, highlighting its value in improving the efficiency of use case modelling across diverse applications. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag London Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2025.},
